WEBVTT

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.360
I think the biggest thing is just recognizing that we're going

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.960
to have AI forever.

00:00:08.093 --> 00:00:11.053
If you don't know how to use it, you're going to fall behind.

00:00:10.993 --> 00:00:15.153
That being said, if that's all you use, you're useless because

00:00:14.853 --> 00:00:16.433
anyone can use AI.

00:00:18.393 --> 00:00:22.313
Like an experience where use of AI has helped you learn something

00:00:22.013 --> 00:00:27.493
or has been productive, a time when it's felt uneasy, unproductive,

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:28.653
or crippling.

00:00:28.673 --> 00:00:31.393
And then what kind of lab culture would you support establishing

00:00:31.093 --> 00:00:32.553
any specifics?

00:00:34.307 --> 00:00:37.877
So Tim's on @claude, can you pick up, move,

00:00:38.187 --> 00:00:40.907
run the following Google Colab code, post here the results,

00:00:40.887 --> 00:00:43.227
and then return the enclosure to its housing.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:47.267
And Claude took 42 minutes, picked it up.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:55.340
so if I had an e-bike I know myself well enough that I would

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.780
probably start relying on the e-bike intensively rather than

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.520
getting a workout that I need every day So it's so much easier

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.680
to be complacent when the tool is there.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.190
It's not like we can choose to not buy the e-bike.

00:01:08.487 --> 00:01:12.807
Oh, sorry — I bought it yeah I bought it for almost like I literally

00:01:12.507 --> 00:01:14.033
bought it for everybody

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:20.120
where Jarvis is a support to Tony Stark and not doing Tony Stark's

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:20.467
work

00:01:22.054 --> 00:01:26.834
We're supposed to be things that act and not to be acted upon

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:32.234
If we start using AI too much then AI starts using us and that

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:33.300
should be the other way around

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.600
give it a baseline.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.680
Like, here's what I'm thinking.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.820
Is there something wrong with what I'm thinking?

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.540
How could this be improved?

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:44.060
I think like people coming into the lab in a semester when things

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.033
have been built, and they're just kind of like,

00:01:46.073 --> 00:01:48.267
I never got the chance to learn any of this.

00:01:48.347 --> 00:01:51.407
Claude has been controlling the whole thing writing the code

00:01:51.107 --> 00:01:54.760
that does the tests and running the tests The scary part of

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.060
that is I have not done that and I don't currently know like

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.740
if you asked me to do it myself I couldn't do it right now it

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.100
would take me a couple days probably that's scary

00:02:03.147 --> 00:02:07.667
It's very good at image analysis spatial analysis things like

00:02:07.367 --> 00:02:09.667
that it's not there yet.

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:13.647
it doesn't live in the 3D world with us it's all just code and

00:02:13.347 --> 00:02:13.907
stuff

00:02:15.713 --> 00:02:18.893
I'm just wondering would it be helpful like when new people

00:02:18.593 --> 00:02:22.513
come into the lab we would run them through hey this is good

00:02:22.213 --> 00:02:27.933
ways to be using AI Maybe like we need some kind of like tutorial

00:02:27.633 --> 00:02:29.500
to show people

00:02:29.547 --> 00:02:33.087
Or maybe I misheard Audrey but I think I heard her say reel

00:02:32.787 --> 00:02:36.327
— so like you know we can make like a reel of us like being

00:02:36.027 --> 00:02:38.400
this is good this is bad

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.300
We'll probably call it good there.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.200
Thanks, Audrey, Carl, everybody.
